INSERT INTO characters (slug,name,team,creator,owner_id,tags,appears_in,data,status,url_slug,created_at,updated_at) VALUES
('doppelganger-the-menagerie','Doppelganger','minion','Goodpart',NULL,NULL,'The Menagerie','{"slug":"doppelganger-the-menagerie","name":"Doppelganger","team":"minion","ability":"1 Townsfolk is poisoned and registers as an evil Minion. You learn a Townsfolk character and register as that character, and good: even if dead. One player knows the Doppelganger is one of two characters. [-1 or +1 Outsider]","creator":"Goodpart","appearsIn":"The Menagerie","quote":"You think I am, therefore I am.","lede":"The Doppelganger steals a good player''s identity and alignment, but leave clues to their true nature.","howToRun":["Remember that the Doppelganger’s role and alignment misregisters, as does the player they replace. This is useful for the Mathematician."],"tips":["You’ve supplanted a Townsfolk: have fun! Although someone around the Grimoire knows you are in play, offering credible information can lend you the credibility necessary to stay alive, and perhaps even implicate the other known player. Remember that because you register falsely, you don’t have to worry about characters like the Dreamer or Ravenkeeper who would otherwise be able to sniff you out, but keep in mind that if you stay alive too long, it’s likely that questions will be asked regarding why. If you’re found out, the world you’ve built might unravel, so prepare a graceful exit while you can.","If a Doppelganger is on script but not in play, you might want to consider being the “knowing” player: this will lead townsfolk to question whether their information is legitimate, or whether their powers are working as intended."],"bluffing":[],"fighting":["The biggest tells as to whether the Doppelganger is in play is that someone finds out at the start of the game. However, this isn’t always reliable, so instead, sniff out double claims. If two players claim the same role and won’t back down, it’s possible that one of them is a Minion. Remember that the one with the poisoned information just might be the real one."],"examples":["Kristin is the Doppelganger who has stolen the Dreamer''s ability. The real Dreamer is poisoned. Kristin is able to learn the roles of Townsfolk, and spread misinformation about the evil team.","Ben is the General who learns that the Empath or the Dentist is the Doppelganger. Ben dies on Night 2. There is a double claim of Dentist, and one is able to correctly discern that the General is dead. The other claims the General is alive. Ben pushes on the Dentist with sober info, who turns out to be the Doppelganger."],"firstNight":13,"firstNightReminder":"Mark one Townsfolk as Duplicated ; note another in-play or out-of-play character as the other character to provide to a player. Wake that player, and tell them that the Doppelganger is the character marked as Duplicated, and the other character.","otherNight":0,"otherNightReminder":"","reminders":["Duplicated","Knows"],"setup":true,"image":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/dope.png","curata":false,"imageAlt":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/dopg.png"}','published','the-menagerie/doppelganger',datetime('now'),datetime('now')),
('efreet','Efreet','minion','Goodpart',NULL,NULL,'The Menagerie','{"slug":"efreet","name":"Efreet","team":"minion","ability":"The first time a Minion dies by execution (not yourself), they become good, but are not told. Choose a player: if good, they turn evil, and learn this. You might register as a different Minion, even if dead.","creator":"Goodpart","appearsIn":"The Menagerie","quote":"You have outlived your usefulness. Make room for another.","lede":"The Efreet keeps a good Minion fighting for evil, turning a good player to the dark side in the process.","howToRun":["Be sure to include the Efreet on scripts that can''t create extra evils. The Efreet creates a good character, but the player turned doesn''t know this - extra evils can turn the voting majority substantially.","Given this shift in evil player count - both real and imagined - be judicious in what you pair the Efreet with.","This Minion requires a fairly familiar group of players, because like a Snake Charmer, the game can turn in an instant, and players may not like feeling as though they''ve had a portion of their agency ripped from them."],"tips":["As the Efreet, you are in an odd position whereby you cannot claim your role to your fellow Minions at first. Feel free to bluff as another Minion, or simply dance around the topic when asked. If you state that you are the Efreet to your team, the first Minion that dies by execution has the opportunity to out your team, so caution is paramount. Instead, continue to sell your bluffed Minion role to your ex-teammate, and know that you have the security of one new evil player, as well as a formerly evil Minion who will continue to act in the interests of your Demon.","Once your ability is spent, you can freely out this information to your other Minions and your Demon, so that they are also aware of a) who their new teammate is, and b) who they can safely dispatch should the need arise.","Another angle to take is, like a Demon pretending to be Snake Charmed, out an evil team that doesn’t exist under the pretence that you were a Minion who was turned good via execution. It’s a risky play, but under the right circumstances, might swing the game in your favour!"],"bluffing":[],"fighting":["Pay attention to players who suddenly shift their allegiances. Like a Mezepheles turned player, Efreet turned players may learn things about the evil team that ultimately lead them to defend players they hadn’t previously, or act in ways that are contrary to their previous behaviours. If you ever have a way of confirming that an Efreet is in play, consider bending the ear of a recently executed Minion to persuade them to fill you in on who the evil players are.","You may also want to look for sudden inconsistencies in information that may point towards an evil turned player: if, for instance, an Empath suddenly changes their numbers, or if a Savant gives provably false statements, there’s a chance they’ve been turned: use that opportunity to work towards a world in which an Efreet has had a hand in proceedings."],"examples":["Laura is the Efreet, claiming to be the Poisoner. The Harpy is executed on Day 3, and turns good that night. The Innkeeper turns evil the same night.","Nathaniel is the Blackmailer, who was executed on Day 2. On Day 4, the sober Village Idiot checks them and sees them as good. Nathaniel realises that there has not been any suggestion of Cerenovus madness, despite hearing that one is in play, and realises they have been turned by the Efreet. They out their evil team. Hooray!"],"firstNight":0,"firstNightReminder":"","otherNight":8.5,"otherNightReminder":"If a Minion was executed during the day and is marked with ''Turns Good'' , change their alignment but do not inform them. Wake the Efreet, and ask them to choose a player. If good, they turn evil. Inform that player of their alignment change.","reminders":["Turns Good","Turns Evil"],"setup":false,"image":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/efre.png","curata":false,"imageAlt":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/efrg.png"}','published','the-menagerie/efreet',datetime('now'),datetime('now')),
('embalmer-the-menagerie','Embalmer','minion','Goodpart',NULL,NULL,'The Menagerie','{"slug":"embalmer-the-menagerie","name":"Embalmer","team":"minion","ability":"Players who die due to another character''s ability register as alive. Once per game*, at night, you may make yourself drunk from now on.","creator":"Goodpart","appearsIn":"The Menagerie","quote":"فَٱلْيَوْمَ نُنَجِّيكَ بِبَدَنِكَ لِتَكُونَ لِمَنْ خَلْفَكَ ءَايَةًۭ ۚ وَإِنَّ كَثِيرًۭا مِّنَ ٱلنَّاسِ عَنْ ءَايَـٰتِنَا.","lede":"The Embalmer suspends players in undeath, leaving uncertainty about who is alive and who is not.","howToRun":["Remember that characters who are dead do not have an ability. Do not wake \"dead\" players during the Night Phase, nor give information to \"dead\" players who would gain information during the day.","Remember that the usual practice of not waking poisoned or drunk players is a \"rule\" you can break with the Embalmer. Poisoned or drunk players who do not wake may think they are dead - but remember that unless a Professor or other reviving role is in play, should the Embalmer ever make themselves drunk or die, they will know this has happened.","If any abilities would cause deaths during the day like the Cerenovus, Psychopath or Golem, don''t hint that they may have happened, as this reveals an Embalmer is definitely in play!","When the Embalmer is drunk, they are drunk from that point on.","If any death would cause the game to end, this still happens."],"tips":["The Barrow-Fog returns - now in Minion form!","As the Embalmer, players who die for reasons other than execution register as alive, blurring the line between life and death, and making the game state uncertain - but the evil team knows exactly what is going on... usually.","If you find an opportune moment to make yourself drunk, be it to frame a specific player who has been saddled with accusations of being the Embalmer, or to frame a specific world where an Embalmer has been made drunk or taken out of play, the big reveal will come with a cost in the form of a swathe of night deaths, sure, but may just secure you enough social credit to coast for the remainder of the game. Be judicious with your ability, and know when to take yourself out of play.","Remember that players who die at night will lose their ability, so don''t claim to wake with information if you''re suggesting you may have died."],"bluffing":[],"fighting":["Ongoing information roles tend to be more difficult kills in Embalmer games. Given that you wake each night, you''ll know exactly when you have died, because you won''t wake anymore. On the flipside, \"spent\" roles, first night roles, or roles with passive abilities are much harder to track, which will cause lots of uncertainty as to the game state. Track when you lose your information, if ever, reverse-engineer the kills from there, and use that information in addition to what you''ve received thus far to sniff out the Embalmer and reveal the game state - permanently."],"examples":["Tania is the Embalmer. Her Demon, the Po, kills 3 people on Night 5, and the Godfather kills on the same night. Tania makes herself drunk, resulting in 6 players suddenly being shown as dead the next morning. Tania claims to be the Engineer who has taken the Embalmer out of play!","Benjamin is the Dentist. The Chef nominates the Virgin, but nothing happens. Benjamin checks the Chef that night and learns a yes, indicating that they likely died to the Virgin, confirming the Embalmer is in play."],"firstNight":0,"firstNightReminder":"","otherNight":30,"otherNightReminder":"Wake the Embalmer and ask if they wish to make themselves drunk. If yes, mark them with the Drunk reminder token .","reminders":["Drunk","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead","Dead"],"setup":false,"jinxes":[{"name":"Lycanthrope","align":"good","text":"The Lycanthrope may choose any player, living or dead, as part of its ability. If they choose a dead player, their kill fails.","id":"lycanthrope"},{"name":"Professor","align":"good","text":"The Professor may choose any player, living or dead, as part of its ability.","id":"professor"},{"name":"Virgin","align":"good","text":"If the Virgin''s ability would execute a Townsfolk, this is not announced and the day does not end.","id":"virgin"},{"name":"Cerenovus","align":"good","text":"If the Cerenovus'' ability would execute a player, this is not announced and the day does not end.","id":"cerenovus"},{"name":"Boomdandy","align":"good","text":"If the Boomdandy is executed, the Embalmer is immediately drunk.","id":"boomdandy"}],"image":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/embe.png","curata":false,"imageAlt":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/embg.png"}','published','the-menagerie/embalmer',datetime('now'),datetime('now')),
('firebug','Firebug','minion','Goodpart',NULL,NULL,'The Menagerie','{"slug":"firebug","name":"Firebug","team":"minion","ability":"On Night 1, a player holds the bomb. Each night*, that player chooses a living player to hold the bomb. If the Demon chooses a player with the bomb, they choose again. You learn evil players holding the bomb.","creator":"Goodpart","appearsIn":"The Menagerie","quote":"They told me I have an explosive personality, and I chose to take that literally.","lede":"The Firebug plays hot potato to give the Demon extra killing power.","howToRun":["Remember that dead players still choose to pass the bomb at night. If they choose a player who is already dead by some other means, prompt them to choose again.","If the Alchemist gains this ability, the subsequent death is at the Storyteller''s discretion: consider killing characters detrimental to town, or even Minions if the game requires rebalancing."],"tips":["The Firebug gets to play with bombs, which can carry serious consequences if they ever go off. Your job is to discern who might be holding the bomb at any given moment, and have your Demon target them in the night to score an extra kill. As an added bonus, if evil players ever hold the bomb, you''ll learn about it, so use that to your advantage and try to co-ordinate your actions.","This character requires a bit of good fortune to be effective, but know that as long as you live, the bomb is out there, somewhere. Pinning down its location will accelerate the game rapidly and lets your Demon sprint to the finish line."],"bluffing":[],"fighting":["While it''s scary to have an explosive hot potato bouncing around town, there is some information to be had in the event that it ever goes off. If you passed the bomb to a player one night, and then the next night a double kill occurs, while it''s possible that the Demon got lucky, there''s also a chance you passed it to an evil player, who co-ordinated the next night with their Demon! Triangulate this information with any other information going around town, and you might be able to pin down the evil team as quickly as they are managing to kill your fellow players. Remember that the Firebug''s power is greatly diminished with no living evil players to bounce off, so if you can secure those vital Minion kills, the Firebug is all but removed from play, as you''ll be able to openly discuss where the bomb will go next."],"examples":["Russell is the Firebug, and the Washerwoman holds the bomb on Night 1. The Washerwoman proceeds to choose the Firebug on Night 2. The Firebug learns this. On Night 3, they pass the bomb to their fellow Minion, who is killed by the Vigormortis the same night. Since the Firebug''s bomb has gone off, the Vigormortis is allowed to choose again.","June is the Oracle. On Night 3, two players die, and June learns a 1. June knows that one of the two players killed was likely a Minion killed by a Vigormortis. In a 9 player game, June knows that no other Minions can hold the bomb, and only the Demon remains as a valid evil bomb holder."],"firstNight":30,"firstNightReminder":"The Firebug chooses a player.","otherNight":30,"otherNightReminder":"Wake the player holding the Firebug''s bomb and inform them they are holding it, and they choose a player. If an evil player is selected, wake the Firebug to inform them.","reminders":["Bomb!"],"setup":false,"jinxes":[{"name":"Alchemist","align":"good","text":"If the Alchemist gains the Firebug''s ability and the Demon chooses a player holding the bomb, the Storyteller makes the Demon''s second choice. The Alchemist does not learn if evil players hold the bomb.","id":"alchemist"},{"name":"Goon","align":"good","text":"If the Goon is chosen to hold the bomb, that player can change the Goon''s alignment and become drunk. The Goon holds the bomb regardless of whether this happens.","id":"goon"},{"name":"Pukka","align":"good","text":"The Pukka does not get to choose again if they choose a player holding the bomb on Night 1.","id":"pukka"}],"image":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/fire.png","curata":false,"imageAlt":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/firg.png"}','published','the-menagerie/firebug',datetime('now'),datetime('now')),
('gerrymanderer','Gerrymanderer','minion','Goodpart',NULL,NULL,'The Menagerie','{"slug":"gerrymanderer","name":"Gerrymanderer","team":"minion","ability":"If 5 or more players live, and 3 nominations failed today, choose a player tonight: they die.","creator":"Goodpart","appearsIn":"The Menagerie","quote":"Stand up, and let every voice be heard! Mine especially.","lede":"The Gerrymanderer doesn''t like nominations that don''t meet the majority, and punishes town for failure.","howToRun":["A tied nomination is also a failed nomination, so make sure to mark those as you would nominations that fall well short.","Giving ample time for nominations allows the Gerrymanderer to operate: consider setting a timer for the nomination process to ensure players know how long they have before day''s end.","Votes for things other than nomination (such as cults or exiles) do not count towards the Gerrymanderer’s tally."],"tips":["You are a Minion that impacts an overlooked aspect of the game: failed nominations. Your job, therefore, is simple. Sow discord, give others cause to nominate, and reap the rewards. Take advantage of roles like Town Crier, Gatekeeper or Flowergirl to rack up failed votes under the guise of gathering information, and reassure town that you are acting in their best interest. You thrive in towns that cannot agree on who to execute, and you are most potent in situations where the evil team isn’t doing the nominating, so consider asking your fellow evil team members to refrain from nominating unless there is a good reason to do so. If you are asked to kill during the night, you can, of course, expedite town’s demise, but you may also choose to hide yourself by killing a dead player, and giving town the impression you don’t exist - until such time that you can maximise your impact late game."],"bluffing":[],"fighting":["If there is a Gerrymanderer in play, you’ll need to be cautious about who votes, and when. Is there a Gatekeeper playing particularly fast and loose? Or perhaps a handful of players who are constantly trying to feed the Town Crier information? There is a chance those players may well be massaging nominations to feed the Gerrymanderer. Consider testing the waters with failed nominations early to see if a Gerrymanderer will show themselves, as that is when they are least damaging. Of course, the best defence against the Gerrymanderer is to nominate decisively and cautiously: keeping failed votes at 2 or less will effectively remove them from play, so watch for players who try to push it over the limit."],"examples":["Travis is the Gerrymanderer. Two players have nominated today, both failures, and so Travis nominates the Savant. Town is forced to put the Savant on the block, or fail and risk the Gerrymanderer killing at night.","Belle is the Librarian who has confirmed a Saint is in play. At 2 nominations today, both failed, Travis nominates the Saint. Belle realises this is the Gerrymanderer trying to push on the Saint at a point where the Gerrymanderer would kill, and so Belle nominates Travis instead."],"firstNight":0,"firstNightReminder":"","otherNight":57,"otherNightReminder":"If the Gerrymanderer has the ‘Democracy Manifest’ reminder token , wake the Gerrymanderer; they select a player.","reminders":["Failed 1st","Failed 2nd","Democracy Manifest"],"setup":false,"image":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/gere.png","curata":false,"imageAlt":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/gerg.png"}','published','the-menagerie/gerrymanderer',datetime('now'),datetime('now')),
('lady-lace','Lady Lace','minion','Goodpart',NULL,NULL,'The Menagerie','{"slug":"lady-lace","name":"Lady Lace","team":"minion","ability":"Each night, choose a player. They are poisoned until you die. Before dawn on the night equal to Minions +1, you die, even if drunk or poisoned.","creator":"Goodpart","appearsIn":"The Menagerie","quote":"Pardon the interruption, but this drink comes courtesy of the fair lady in the red dress. She sends her warmest regards.","lede":"Lady Lace causes multiple players to be poisoned throughout the game, dying a dramatic death not long after.","howToRun":["Remember to set a note regarding Lady Lace’s time of death! Without it, you may miss the chance to kill Lady Lace when you should, and potentially corrupt town’s information."],"tips":["You are a Poisoner with greater potency by far, but unlike the Poisoner, you are on borrowed time: maximise your effect by sniping high-value roles, and cause as much chaos as you can before your time is up.","Given that you must die after a certain number of nights, you can coordinate with your Demon such that it might cover your demise: have the Demon kill you in the night, or sell a situation where an Assassin, Gossip, or other non-Demon death occurred.","If no such conditions exist in the game, you may also want to consider putting yourself up for execution before your time expires: though this is a loss for the evil team, and your poisoning will be reversed, at least you’ll have covered your tracks such that there may be no obvious sign that you were ever in play."],"bluffing":[],"fighting":["If Lady Lace is on a script, consider making a note of who dies on the night that corresponds to when Lady Lace should die: there is a good chance that if nothing seems irregular, the evil team just lost one of their Minions. Reverse engineer the information you have on hand to work out who was poisoned, and when, and you’ll be able to determine what information is trustworthy."],"examples":["Penny is Lady Lace. She poisons the Chef on Night 1, the Empath on Night 2, and the Gambler on Night 3. The Demon kills an already dead player. On Day 3, the Storyteller announces that Penny - and only Penny - is dead.","Murphy is the Empath who is poisoned on Night 1, receiving a zero. His neighbours do not change. On Night 4, he receives a 1. Murphy can infer that Penny, who died on Night 3, may have been Lady Lace."],"firstNight":29,"firstNightReminder":"Lady Lace chooses a player . Mark Lady Lace with the token corresponding to the night equal to Minions +1.","otherNight":15,"otherNightReminder":"Lady Lace chooses a player . If the night number corresponds with the token currently on Lady Lace, they die at the conclusion of the night and all poison effects are removed.","reminders":["Dies Night 2","Dies Night 3","Dies Night 4","Poisoned","Poisoned","Poisoned","Poisoned"],"setup":false,"jinxes":[{"name":"Preacher","align":"good","text":"Lady Lace still dies if their time expires while under the effects of the Preacher’s ability.","id":"preacher"},{"name":"Plague Doctor","align":"good","text":"If the Storyteller gains Lady Lace’s ability, they may poison 2 players on subsequent nights: on the 3rd night, all effects are removed.","id":"plaguedoctor"},{"name":"Thaumaturge","align":"good","text":"Lady Lace still dies if their time expires while poisoned due to the Thaumaturge''s ability.","slug":"thaumaturge-the-menagerie"}],"image":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/lade.png","curata":false,"imageAlt":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/ladg.png"}','published','the-menagerie/lady-lace',datetime('now'),datetime('now')),
('muckraker','Muckraker','minion','Goodpart',NULL,NULL,'The Menagerie','{"slug":"muckraker","name":"Muckraker","team":"minion","ability":"Each day, 3 living players must make a different public statement about the game, or good loses. Tonight, if 1 statement was false, a player dies.","creator":"Goodpart","appearsIn":"The Menagerie","quote":"Sir, please. It''s right there, in print, clear as day. ''MAYORAL SCANDAL - CAVORTING WITH DEMONS'' - save your lies for the courts.","lede":"The Muckraker thrives off scandal, causing death at night due to false declarations about the town.","howToRun":["As with the Gossip, consider what would be termed a “valid” statement. “X is good” is a valid statement, for instance, but something like “X is not not good” is not a validly different statement. Statements that refer to situations outside the game, or joke statements don’t count. When taking statements, preference those with their hands up first where possible, but if players are eager, try to select different players each time so that everyone has a turn to prove themselves. If the Muckraker dies, continue to take statements as per normal.","If a Muckraker is drunk or preached on its first day, its presence is not announced to the town until its power is active."],"tips":["Unlike the Gossip, who causes deaths on the basis of true statements, the Muckraker causes deaths if any or all of the statements are false. As such, make claims that sound plausible, but ultimately turn out to be untrue, and encourage your teammates to do the same. Draw suspicion to the statements that are made, puzzle out how you may be able to twist them to your advantage: and if not, enjoy free information about the town to help hone your Demon’s kills! Also contrary to the Gossip’s ability, for as long as your ability is in play, players must make Muckraker statements, so there is no opting out when the game state becomes tenuous.","You may also want to consider poisoning the Muckraker if possible and jumping in with an untrue statement that sells false worlds, but remember that the Muckraker has to be poisoned at the point they would activate in the night."],"bluffing":[],"fighting":["Given that there are things you know to be true about the game, feel free to jump in and share them with town to fulfill the requirements of the Muckraker, but do remember that you may feed valuable information to the evil team in the process. Keep your statements vague enough to be valid, or reveal information that is already public. Consider tracking Muckraker statements to pull together an understanding of the world, and puzzle out which statements are false (and who is making them!)"],"examples":["James is the Muckraker. On Day 1, three good players make true statements, and no-one dies. On Day 2, a good player makes a false statement, and someone dies at night. On Night 4, James is poisoned: during the day, James makes a false statement about their Demon''s alignment. No-one dies at night.","Marta is the Noble, who claims on Day 1 that their Noble pings are a valid Noble ping; two other players declare that a Muckraker is in play, and an Imp (the only Demon on script) is in play. Someone dies at night: Marta infers that she was poisoned on Night 1."],"firstNight":0,"firstNightReminder":"","otherNight":57,"otherNightReminder":"If a player has made an incorrect statement for the Muckraker, the player marked ''Slandered'' dies.","reminders":["Slandered"],"setup":false,"image":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/muce.png","curata":false,"imageAlt":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/mucg.png"}','published','the-menagerie/muckraker',datetime('now'),datetime('now')),
('necromancer-the-menagerie','Necromancer','minion','Goodpart',NULL,NULL,'The Menagerie','{"slug":"necromancer-the-menagerie","name":"Necromancer","team":"minion","ability":"Once per game, at night*, choose a dead player (not a Demon). You gain their ability.","creator":"Goodpart","appearsIn":"The Menagerie","quote":"Ⱄⱆⱃⰳⰵ Ⰵⱅ Ⰿⰻⱀⰻⱄⱅⱃⰰ Ⰿⰻⱈⰻ... Ⱄⱆⱃⰳⰵ Ⰵⱅ Ⰿⰻⱀⰻⱄⱅⱃⰰ Ⰿⰻⱈⰻ...","lede":"The Necromancer steals abilities from dead players.","howToRun":["If the Necromancer''s chosen player is revived by some means, the Necromancer loses their ability."],"tips":["While the obvious choice for a Necromancer is to gain the ability of a dead Minion, this isn''t necessarily your only option. Perhaps you can steal the Savant''s ability and gain statements about the game during the day? Or perhaps you''d rather become the Dreamer, learning about player''s roles? The Necromancer is a versatile Minion that can be used for information gathering, or to keep Minion abilities in play, so the choice is up to you."],"bluffing":[],"fighting":["You''ve confirmed that town has killed the Fearmonger, but the next night, the Fearmonger has chosen a new player? It must be the Necromancer!","Confirming that Minions have died is a surefire way to determine that a Necromancer is about. If you can determine that, then you know which Minions are in play, and you know there''s far less to fear than before.","Be equally wary of players who claim the roles of dead players: while those dead players may have been bluffing their role, it''s equally possible that a Necromancer has stolen their ability to gain credibility. Discerning the difference is the best way to figure out whether a Necromancer is on the loose or not."],"examples":["Zak is the Necromancer. He selects the Witch who died to their own ability, and gains that power from that point on.","Susan is the Nightwatchman. There is no double claim of their role, but upon their death on Day 3, another player claims to have received a Nightwatchman''s call. Susan can infer that a Necromancer has assumed her ability."],"firstNight":0,"firstNightReminder":"","otherNight":40,"otherNightReminder":"The Necromancer chooses a player. Mark that player with the ''Raised'' reminder token . From then on, wake the Necromancer at the point in the night order corresponding to that character.","reminders":["Raised"],"setup":false,"jinxes":[{"name":"Virgin","align":"good","text":"If the Necromancer gains the Virgin''s ability and 5 or less players live (Travellers don''t count), the Necromancer''s ability fails.","id":"virgin"},{"name":"Golem","align":"good","text":"If the Necromancer gains the Golem''s ability and 5 or less players live (Travellers don''t count), the Necromancer''s ability fails.","id":"golem"}],"image":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/nece.png","curata":false,"imageAlt":"https://menagerie.towerrangers.net/images/necg.png"}','published','the-menagerie/necromancer',datetime('now'),datetime('now'));